{"title":"Israel's prince of public diplomacy (Globes English | Israel Business Arena, 2023-12-03T12:50:26+00:00)","text":"Israel's prince of public diplomacy (Globes English | Israel Business Arena, 2023-12-03T12:50:26+00:00)
Everyone knows that even if they are not affiliated with Hamas, they are not free to present the full truth, because they are not allowed to. There is not a single photo of a terrorist, there is not a single photo of a missile being launched from a densely populated area, from a mosque or a school, not even a single photo from the Hamas tunnels under Shifa. Why? Because these reporters, even if they are not explicitly on the side of Hamas, operate under a dictatorial regime and cannot provide the information even if they wanted to. And it is very important to continue reminding the world how much the information that reaches them is biased and does not reflect reality."

Despite all this, Levy feels he must stress that not everyone is against us. "I don't want to give the impression that all the interviewers are hostile and every question is delusional. Absolutely not. I have many interviews with reporters who understand what is happening here and simply want to get information and hear the Israeli side, which is completely legitimate."","entities":["Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Hamas","public diplomacy","Israel's prince of public diplomacy","Shifa","Levy","Israel"],"facts":["Israel's defense undone by reliance on technology -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena",""There are no sacred numbers" -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","There are no sacred numbers -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Office rents in Israel stay firm - for now -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena","Psagot worth 80% less since Rani Zim bought it -PUBLISHED_BY-> Globes English | Israel Business Arena"]}

{"title":"The airline parts scandal sparking panic and introspection in aerospace (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-10-16T20:58:51+00:00)","text":"The airline parts scandal sparking panic and introspection in aerospace (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-10-16T20:58:51+00:00)
His company is accused of falsifying the records that come with aviation components to show they are the real deal. CFM, a joint venture between GE and Safran that manufactures engines, is now pursuing a legal case against AOG in Britain’s High Court. Zamora is defending the case. Verification documents are important because of the exacting specifications of airline parts. Engine modules must tolerate temperatures that would melt many materials and even basic parts must be certified. Because of these demands, even a bolt can cost thousands of dollars if it is holding an engine in place. As a result, the market for airline parts is a very lucrative one. Since 2015, Zamora has charmed his way deep into the complex network of companies and decision makers who move billions of dollars worth of airline parts around the world.

Business associates who worked with him outside AOG and who were willing to speak to The Telegraph describe a savvy operator who was fun to be around.","entities":["The Sydney Morning Herald","CFM","GE","Safran","Britain","Britain’s High Court","legal case","engines","airline parts","falsifying records","Zamora","AOG","The airline parts scandal sparking panic and introspection in aerospace","The Telegraph"],"facts":["ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to open lower as Wall Street closes September with more losses -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","The best shows to stream in December -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","McRae reveals hidden message he carried under collar of his grand final shirt -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald","ASX set to drop as Wall Street’s September slump deepens -PUBLISHED_BY-> The Sydney Morning Herald"]}

