“This is another indication that AI is progressing faster than even many experts anticipated,” Victoria Krakovna, who would soon join DeepMind as an “AI safety” researcher, wrote in a blog post. DeepMind’s founders were increasingly worried about what Google would do with their inventions. In 2017, they tried to break away from the company. Google responded by increasing the salaries and stock award packages of the DeepMind founders and their staff. They stayed put. The ethics board never had a second meeting. The Breakup

Convinced that Page’s optimistic view of AI was dead wrong, and angry at his loss of DeepMind, Musk built his own lab. OpenAI was founded in late 2015, just a few months after he met with Altman at the Rosewood hotel in Silicon Valley. Sam Altman Credit: Bloomberg Businessweek Musk pumped money into the lab, and his former PayPal buddies – Hoffman and Thiel – came along for the ride.","entities":["Musk","Page","Rosewood hotel","Thiel","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","Victoria Krakovna","Bloomberg Businessweek","Hoffman","PayPal","ethics board","break away from Google","DeepMind","The Age","Google","Silicon Valley"],"facts":["Why Mozilla is betting on a decentralized social networking future -MENTIONS-> Musk","After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> Musk","Founders, are events useful? -MENTIONS-> Musk","X (formerly Twitter) is worst for disinformation, per EU analysis -MENTIONS-> Musk","The UK just laid out new rules for the internet — it only gets harder from here -MENTIONS-> Musk"]}

{"title":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)","text":"How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence (The Age, 2023-12-09T18:30:00+00:00)
When Google bought DeepMind, it bought the whole thing. Musk was out. Financially, he had come out ahead, but he was unhappy. Three Google executives now firmly in control of DeepMind were there: Page; Sergey Brin, a Google co-founder and Tesla investor; and Eric Schmidt, Google’s chair. Among the other attendees were Reid Hoffman, another PayPal founder; and Toby Ord, an Australian philosopher studying “existential risk”.

The DeepMind founders reported that they were pushing ahead with their work but that they were aware the technology carried serious risks. Suleyman, the DeepMind co-founder, gave a presentation called “The Pitchforkers Are Coming.” AI could lead to an explosion in disinformation, he told the board. He fretted that as the technology replaced countless jobs in the coming years, the public would accuse Google of stealing their livelihoods. Google would need to share its wealth with the millions who could no longer find work and provide a “universal basic income,” he argued. Musk agreed.","entities":["disinformation","Musk","Page","How ego and fear fuelled the rise of artificial intelligence","Sergey Brin","Toby Ord","Suleyman","The Pitchforkers Are Coming","universal basic income","jobs","PayPal","DeepMind","existential risk","The Age","Google"],"facts":["After warning, the EU opens investigation into X over alleged disinformation -MENTIONS-> disinformation","EU fires urgent warning at Elon Musk’s X over illegal content and disinformation following Hamas attacks -MENTIONS-> disinformation","The Israel-Hamas War Is Drowning X in Disinformation -MENTIONS-> disinformation","One year later, ChatGPT is still alive and kicking -MENTIONS-> disinformation","How the conspiracy-fueled Epoch Times went mainstream and made millions -MENTIONS-> disinformation"]}

